    "turn_text": "the crop revenue option or shallow losses. Some people like to refer to it. They'll have price protection. What you would refer to is taking off a reference prices. They have to make a decision about which way to go. either program potentially if we have whether or market failures has an impact on the u.s. treasury but the thing i think we all agreed in and two-thirds of the how well two-thirds of the senate and overwhelming majority of the house was need safety net and if things really really really get bad enough can and then we're going to pay out some resources but then why do you well by life insurance policy you don't want to die but you want to make sure your loved ones are taken care of why do you buy fire insurance policy on your house you want to live there for thirty forty fifty years and never file a claim, but you spend the money. That's my perspective on the safety net in this farm bill. If markets are consistent, if other nature cooperates in some semblance of a way, then I think there's a high probability most of these things will not be used. But if we need them, it'll be because things have happened beyond the control of a farmer and rancher and they'll really need them. I guess I would say there's always a potential for exposure, but the biggest financial exposure in the Farm Bill to the American taxpayer is still not the commodity title. It's still not the conservation title. It's the nutrition title where almost 80% of the recent years all the resources have gone.",
