    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "It's a bold new concept shifting away from the MLIC and going over to in fact an insurance system to help cover the cost of potential cost of production. When both members from the northeast where dairies typically are relatively small by modern standards and members from places like California where There is tend to be rather substantial by anybody's definition when they all sign the conference report at the same time. And that indicated to me that there was an optimism of hope. The speaker of the United States House, one of the very few guidelines he laid down early on the farm bill process was that we could not have supply management. You could not have a farm bill where somebody at USDA was telling a dairyman to turn the valve on or the valve off at his or her milk tank. We avoid that. And in the policy in place, if production gets out of hand, the premiums you pay in to get a benefit out of the program. And at a point, the benefits themselves as the premiums go up. What you pay in goes up and what you get out goes down is to an effort to provide a disincentive to encourage people to be rational in their decisions about production. Once again, teeing off of the concepts of insurance. Healthier lifestyle you lead, usually the cheaper personal insurance is. The less healthier lifestyle, the more you're going to pay. Kind of the way I hope the dairy language ultimately",
