  {
    "episode_id": 72820,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72820.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "When my constituents come to understand that we spent $23 billion less in this farm bill than was spent in the previous farm bill. When my constituents come to understand that we achieved the first real reform in the food stamp program since 1996, an $8 billion savings by ending the LIHEAP or Heat&eat hole that persuaded the Senate and the president to accept the bill that in 10 states sets up a pilot project to require able-bodied adults to work after work to get their food stamps. When they realize we persuaded the president and the Senate to accept the bill that ends advertising on food stamps, ends recruiting of people to push food stamps, in the pushing food stamps in foreign countries. That ends people getting food stamps in multiple states simultaneously. That ends so many abuses. And at the same time, ends the old direct payment program that was so criticized and moves us all to an insurance-based program. Provides a safety net that I think is as good as or perhaps better than the previous farm bill for less. I don't know what conservative standard that doesn't meet. Spin less, do more, do it more efficiently, require more of people. I just, I don't know, but can we live in a funny time? And sound bites are the word. I worked for two and a half years with my colleagues, the craft a bill that's about 20 or 30% in pages smaller than the previous Farm Bill, actually a smaller document than the previous one. I believe, on my neighbors back home, we'll look past the political pitches of the super PACs on the east and west coast, and we'll look past the professional political pressure groups on the east and west coast. They'll understand I did my work in a way that reflects what they wanted.",
